   "question": "Why did Industrials revenue miss plan in Q3?",
   "answer": "**Headline:** Industrials Q3 revenue missed plan by **$8.8\u202fM**, driven almost entirely by an M&A Advisory shortfall.\n\n**Drivers**\n- M&A Advisory product: **\u2011$7.5\u202fM** variance, **85\u202f%** of the total gap.  \n- Lending product: **\u2011$1.3\u202fM** variance, **15\u202f%** of the total gap.  \n- Meadowlark Fabrication client: **\u2011$4.6\u202fM** variance, **52\u202f%** of the gap.  \n- Thornbury Components client: **\u2011$3.0\u202fM** variance, **34\u202f%** of the gap.  \n\n**Executive paragraph**  \nThe Industrials segment fell **$8.8\u202fM** short of its Q3 budget, a miss of **\u201119\u202f%**. The bulk of the shortfall stems from M&A Advisory revenue, which recorded zero bookings versus a $7.5\u202fM plan, accounting for **85\u202f%** of the variance. Two slipped M&A deals\u2014Meadowlark Fabrication ($4.5\u202fM) and Thornbury Components ($3.0\u202fM)\u2014closed after the quarter, turning expected fees into timing variance. Lending contributed the remaining gap.\n\n**Definitions used**\n- Revenue vs Plan: How far booked revenue is above or below the approved plan; a negative variance is a miss. (Source: CIB Business Definitions Glossary \u203a Revenue vs Plan)  \n- Variance driver: A product, client, etc., ranked by how much of the total revenue gap it explains. (Source: CIB Business Definitions Glossary \u203a Variance driver)  \n- Slipped deal: A deal expected to close in a period but closed later, causing a timing variance. (Source: CIB Business Definitions Glossary \u203a Slipped deal)\n\n_Synthetic data \u00b7 Northlake Bank (fictional). Data as of 2026-11-24._",
